  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by an\nupper-low across central Arizona slowly lifting to the northeast,\na strong subtropical ridge centered across SERN Texas, a trough\nof low pressure along the British Columbia Canada coastline, \nand an active tropical pattern over the Eastern Pacific. This is\nthe latest information from the NHC's on Hurricane Polo (category\n4 with maximum sustained winds of 150 mph near 17.1N/106.5W \nmoving west at 10 kts), and Hurricane Odalys (category 2 with \nmaximum sustained winds 110 mph near 17.1N/124.2W moving ENE at \n7 kts).\n\nIn the short term, the upper-low that is over central Arizona \nhas been responsible for early morning convection across Gila\nand southern Navajo counties. This system is expected to eject \nnortheast this morning as the upper ridge over SERN Texas \nflattens out and broadens across northern Mexico. This will \nleave Southeast Arizona on the subsident side of the upper-low \ntoday, inhibiting shower and thunderstorm activity across all \nbut the White Mountains. In addition, we will see drier air \nspread out across our neck of the woods, with precipitable water\nvalues expected to dip to 0.50-0.75 inches SE of Tucson, and \n0.75-1.00 inches to the northwest. Both of these conditions will\nleave Southeast Arizona quiet for the next 48-60 hours.\n\nConditions begin to get interesting Sunday when Hurricane \nOdalys weakens to a remnant low near 23N/123W and shears out \naloft into the Desert Southwest. Although this will increase \nPWATs into the 1.00-1.50 inch range in SE AZ, this will mainly \noccur in the mid/upper levels resulting in significant mid and \nhigh clouds covering Southeast Arizona along with a few light \nshowers west of Tucson Sunday afternoon/evening. The more \nimportant feature of note in this forecast is Hurricane Polo. \nIt is progged to move west today, northwest Saturday, north \nSunday, then eventually recurve to the northeast and across the \nsouthern half of the Baja Monday and Tuesday. This track will \nhelp push some tropical moisture north up the Gulf of California\nSunday and across southern Arizona both Monday and Tuesday. In \nfact, both the 25/00Z GEFS and ECMWF ensemble means indicate a \nplume of moist PWATs 2.5-3.0 standard deviations above normal \nacross Southeast Arizona 29/00Z Monday afternoon.\n\nAlthough we will see a deep plume of tropical moisture across \nour neck of the woods with the assist by Polo, it is important \nto note that the remains of Polo are NOT expected to impact us \ndirectly. A trough positioned along the British Columbia \ncoastline this morning will dig south along the West Coast \nthrough Monday morning, then swing east across Arizona as a \nclosed upper-low Monday afternoon through Tuesday night. Given \nthe deep moisture in place, this feature will result in \nwidespread showers and thunderstorms across Southeast Arizona. \nModest deformation in the flow aloft Monday will support mainly \nlight to moderate intensity rainfall with pockets of heavy rain,\nwith the potential for more widespread moderate to heavy \nrainfall Tuesday with stronger dynamics along the trough axis \ntracking across the area. In fact, strong 0-6km bulk shear will \nbe present Tuesday with the potential for a few mini-supercells \nto be embedded within the widespread storm activity Tuesday. In \nany event, looks like a busy period ahead the first half of next\nweek.",
